Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 17:39:21 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] WIP: arm64: dts: qcom: sm6450: Give the USB HS PHY its
 reference clock gate
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The HS PHY's reference clock reaches it through GCC_EUSB3_0_CLKREF_EN,
which the stock tree hands this PHY as its ref_clk, and the EUD as its
clkref. The PHY only took CXO, so nothing in Linux held that gate: it
stayed on because the bootloader left it on.

The card logs of rounds 37, 52 and 57, the three rounds that booted the
ADSP, all end with the gate off in hardware, and the USB link went with
it each time. Every round without the ADSP, up to 1138 s of uptime, has
it on. Something outside Linux clears it as the ADSP comes up.

Holding it from the PHY at least means a PHY init turns it back on.
Whether holding it also keeps it on is what round 60 asks.

To be folded into "arm64: dts: qcom: Add SM6450 SoC device tree" once
round 60 shows what the gate does.

Signed-off-by: Bruno Gregório <accounts@bruno.pw>
(cherry picked from commit 11413e0eac5afce36854cd58389dfbffea495903)
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6450.dtsi | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6450.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6450.dtsi
index 029a11748..c721d064c 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6450.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6450.dtsi
@@ -1120,7 +1120,7 @@ usb_1_hsphy: phy@88e3000 {
 				     "qcom,usb-snps-hs-7nm-phy";
 			reg = <0x0 0x088e3000 0x0 0x400>;
 
-			clocks = <&rpmhcc RPMH_CXO_CLK>;
+			clocks = <&gcc GCC_EUSB3_0_CLKREF_EN>;
 			clock-names = "ref";
 			resets = <&gcc GCC_QUSB2PHY_PRIM_BCR>;
 
